        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Mir 25 mission began on January 31 after the Soyuz TM-27 spacecraft docked with the Mir Space Station. The crew consisted of French European Space Agency (ESA) cosmonaut Leopold Eyharts and Russian Space Agency (RSA) cosmonauts, Commander Talgat Musabayev and Flight Engineer Nikolai Budarin.  After conducting a variety of space science experiments during his 3-week stay aboard the station, the ESA cosmonaut returned to Earth with the Mir 24 crew.<P>On January 24, 1998, just prior to the arrival of the Mir 25 crew, the Space Shuttle Endeavour (STS-89) brought the NASA 7 crewmember to replace the NASA 6 crewmember on board the station.  Science conducted aboard the station was in various research areas, including human life sciences, fundamental biology to study animals and plants, biotechnology, materials sciences, and spacecraft structural dynamics and environment. Results from these studies assisted researchers in developing the operating procedures on board the International Space Station (ISS).<P>The Russian cosmonauts performed several extravehicular activities (EVAs) to make further repairs to the space station.  On April 1 and April 6, they attached braces to the damaged solar array on the Spektr Module.  On April 11, 17 and 22, they replaced an attitude control system that was located atop a 50-foot tower attached to Mir.  The NASA 7 crewmember supported these spacewalks from inside the station.<P>Returning to Earth on June 12, 1998 aboard the Shuttle Discovery (STS-91), the NASA 7 crewmember was the last U.S. astronaut to complete a long-duration mission on Mir.<P>On August 15, 1998, the Russian Mir 26 crew arrived at the Mir Space Station to replace the Mir 25 crew.  On August 25, 1998, the Mir 25 crew returned to Earth. <END>
        </div>

